What npm run db:migrate does
It runs TypeORM's migration runner against your Postgres database to bring the schema (tables, indexes, foreign keys, extensions) up to date.

The full chain expands like this:


npm run db:migrate
  → npm run typeorm -- migration:run
  → typeorm-ts-node-commonjs -d ./src/database/data-source.ts migration:run
It uses the same data-source.ts as db:seed (same DB_HOST / DB_PORT / DB_USER / DB_PASSWORD / DB_NAME env vars), scans backend/src/database/migrations/ for migration files, and:

Creates a migrations tracking table the first time it runs (records which migrations have been applied, with timestamps).
Looks at every migration class on disk, compares its timestamp prefix against the tracking table.
Runs the up() method of each unapplied migration in timestamp order, inside a transaction.
Inserts a row into migrations for each one that succeeded, so it won't run again next time.
What this project currently has
One migration: 1715800000000-initial-schema.ts. On a fresh DB it:

Enables the pgcrypto extension (for gen_random_uuid()).
Creates 19 tables in FK-dependency order:
Auth/users: users, refresh_tokens
Catalog content: personas, scenarios, courses, course_scenarios
Conversation runtime: conversation_sessions, conversation_messages, session_scores
Progress tracking: skill_snapshots, user_progress, user_scenario_completions
Achievements: achievements, user_achievements
Moderation/admin: guard_violations, app_config, admin_audit_log, admin_permissions, uploaded_files
Adds all the supporting indexes (XP/streak leaderboards, status partials, FK indexes, the uniq_one_superadmin partial unique index, etc.).
Defines a down() that drops everything in reverse FK order — that's what npm run db:migrate:revert triggers.
Typical lifecycle

db:up         # docker compose up postgres
db:migrate    # create tables (this command)
db:seed       # fill catalog content (personas, scenarios, courses, …)
Related scripts
db:migrate:revert — runs the last applied migration's down() and removes its row from migrations. One step back at a time.
db:migrate:generate — diffs your entities against the live DB schema and writes a new migration file with the SQL needed to close the gap. You'd use this after editing an entity; review the generated file before checking it in.
What it does NOT do
Doesn't insert any data — that's db:seed's job.
Doesn't drop or recreate tables that already exist — migration files are append-only history.
Doesn't auto-detect entity changes — you have to run db:migrate:generate (or hand-write a new migration) for the runner to have something new to apply.